--TEST--
Every `Core\Db\Row` member takes its column as a `string` value the caller computes, and a column *number* is not a spelling of one — `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The argument side of the same roster the other three cases pin from the
// return side. Two claims, and they are one boundary asserted on both halves:
// a column name is an ordinary `string` **value**, so a variable works
// everywhere a literal does; and it is *only* a name, so the positional
// reading PHP's `FETCH_NUM` and `fetchColumn($n)` offer has no spelling here at
// all. `Core\Db\Rows::column` is where a position is taken, and it is a
// different member on a different class for exactly that reason.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $rows = $db->query("select * from accounts", []);

// Not a literal at the call site. Nothing about these members is resolved at
// compile time from the name, which is what makes a column list a program can
// build possible at all.
var $name = "owner";

// The position half, on the member that has one: `column` takes `int|string`,
// so this is the accepted spelling of "the first column of every row".
array<mixed> $byPosition = $rows->column(0);

foreach ($rows->all() as Core\Db\Row $row) {
    var $present = $row->has($name);
    var $any = $row->get($name);
    var $whole = $row->toArray();
    var $owner = $row->string($name);
    var $photo = $row->bytes($name);
    var $id = $row->int($name);
    var $seq = $row->uint($name);
    var $ratio = $row->float($name);
    var $active = $row->bool($name);
    var $balance = $row->decimal($name);
    var $seen = $row->instant($name);
    var $born = $row->date($name);
    var $opens = $row->time($name);
    var $external = $row->uuid($name);

    // And the refusal, which is the boundary: a reader takes the label and
    // nothing else. A member that also accepted a number would make
    // `$row->int(0)` and `$row->int("0")` two different questions with one
    // spelling, which is the ambiguity `Rows::column` above keeps on its own
    // member instead.
    var $refused = $row->int(1);
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `int`
   --> case.nvs:42:30
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
